#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ shown above. If\n${x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D$\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/f62ea4c35fcc7d18eac85d6d253ef727.png)\n\n1. $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n2. $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n3. $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define\n\n$$\n{x^{2} - y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D\n$$\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} {:answer/id #uuid "e565a422-78a5-5156-95c3-37f3e40e3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151382} {:answer/id #uuid "2784e1ac-6059-539c-84d8-e18877b6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151383} {:answer/id #uuid "394eb603-5869-540e-a54b-b403c5a2c6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151384} {:answer/id #uuid "704cc418-5f2d-5e6f-a447-641d3ab76e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc27ba3-e52d-57f2-a804-44b6bc1596df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151380}] :question/id #uuid "0e648a84-4fc0-5e89-9abf-3bfad9d2cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-80266" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1$. If\n$f(x, y) = {xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖Dand∬_(D)xydA = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R$?\n\n1. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$\n2. $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$\n3. $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$"}]}
